  "synthesis": {
    "description": "Marketing and facility planning work up proposed pipeline interconnects and expansions. A request for an interconnect arrives and is handed to a marketing contact with a planning counterpart; facility planning engineers then produce cost estimates, listing the work needed to make an interconnect bi-directional, pressure ranges and spreadsheets of build costs, sometimes split out for interest, overheads and AFUDC at marketing's request. Maps and facility data are gathered from field staff, and meetings are set to discuss pressure scenarios and facility modifications with the customer.",
    "steps": [
      "A customer or marketing raises a proposed interconnect or expansion project.",
      "A marketing contact and a facility planning counterpart are named for the request.",
      "Planning gathers facility information and maps from field offices.",
      "Engineers prepare a cost estimate and the list of work required, with pressure ranges.",
      "The estimate spreadsheet is reworked to marketing's requested layout and sent on.",
      "Marketing schedules a meeting with the customer to review pressures, modifications and costs."
    ],
    "approach": "Use a standard interconnect request intake that names the marketing and planning owners at the start, and a cost estimate spreadsheet layout that already breaks out interest, overheads and AFUDC by line item along with pressure ranges, so estimates arrive in the form marketing needs without a second pass.",
    "rationale": "The repeated effort is assembling cost estimates and reformatting them for marketing; a fixed estimate layout and intake form addresses both the reformatting request and the owner confusion, while the engineering judgement stays with planning.",
    "artifact_type": "template"
  },
